= The question behind the rescue
Federal support paid for approximately 56% of academic basic research in the United States in 2023 @nsb2025. Decisions in Washington therefore reach well into the universities where research is done. In 2025, the National Science Foundation (NSF) listed more than 1,600 terminated awards as of June 5, citing changed agency priorities @gaonsf2025[appendix IV]. These decisions interrupted support that had already been approved, putting continuity at issue alongside the size of future budgets. As a physics student working in cosmology, I have a stake in whether federal commitments last long enough for researchers to carry out their work.

Peer-reviewed research gives Congress a reason to take that continuity seriously. Studying earlier delays in National Institutes of Health (NIH) grant renewals, #cite(<tham2026>, form: "prose", supplement: [abstract]) estimate that interruptions of at least 30 days increased nonemployment by three percentage points among personnel in laboratories supported by a single R01 grant. The study concerns NIH renewals, while the NSF episode concerns terminations. Its employment finding explains why the timing and reliability of public support deserve attention: a gap in funding can reach into a researcher's career.

I argue that Congress should justify NSF funding in terms of the research objectives it intends to sustain, then require public reporting and 30 days’ notice before category-wide grant cancellations. A statutory bar on implementing those cancellations during the notice period would give Congress time to examine the decision and affected teams time to seek alternative support. The FY2026 settlement protected NSF against most of the president's proposed contraction, yet left it with less purchasing power than a decade earlier and substantially less funding than Congress had authorized for expansion. A successful budget negotiation is therefore an inadequate standard of protection: it lets the severity of the president's proposal define success.

NSF is the principal case, examined over FY2017–FY2026. NASA provides a brief comparison of priorities within an agency, while the grant terminations expose the difference between allocating money and overseeing its use. The policy question is how Congress can make a claim of protection answerable to the research it says it supports.

= What Congress saved
The course's treatment of Congress distinguishes its budgetary function from its oversight of the executive branch @coursecongress2026[secs.~6.5.2–6.5.3]. Article I's Appropriations Clause grounds Congress's spending power: Treasury withdrawals require appropriations made by law @constitution. The president proposes a budget, Congress supplies budget authority, and agencies use that authority to incur obligations and make payments. An appropriation therefore establishes resources agencies may use; its passage is an exercise of legislative power rather than approval of a presidential decision already made.

For FY2026, the president requested approximately \$3.903 billion for NSF, compared with \$9.060 billion enacted for FY2025 @harris2026[table 1]. Congress instead provided \$8.750 billion in the law signed on January 23, 2026 @congress2026. Calculated against the FY2025 enacted baseline, the request would have reduced funding by 56.9%; the final appropriation reduced it by 3.4%. Congress offset approximately 94.0% of the proposed reduction. That was a substantial check on the administration's budget agenda, leaving NSF with nearly \$4.85 billion more than the president had requested.

The comparison becomes more favorable to Congress when it uses the amount actually available in FY2025. The enacted total included \$234 million in emergency construction funding that the president did not make available, leaving an adjusted baseline of \$8.826 billion @harris2026[pp.~2–4]. Relative to that amount, FY2026 funding fell by only 0.9%. Congress preserved nearly the previous year's nominal resources. Recognizing that achievement is necessary to assess its limits fairly.

The public arguments for the settlement reflected different political commitments. Senators Susan Collins and Jerry Moran emphasized competitiveness and benefits to constituents, while Senator Patty Murray emphasized resisting the administration's cuts @senate2026 @murray2026. Both positions gave legislators reasons to oppose the request. Neither, by itself, answers how the surviving appropriation compares with NSF's needs over the longer term. For that question, the president's proposed number is too movable a reference point.

= A decade of purchasing power
@fig-gap illustrates the problem. Appropriations exceeded the president's request in every fiscal year from 2018 through 2021; they fell below the request in every year from 2022 through 2025 @harris2026[table A-1]. A comparison with the request describes the outcome of a particular budget contest. The underlying funding record needs a measure that remains meaningful when the administration's ambitions change.

I assembled ten fiscal years of NSF funding from Congressional Research Service (CRS) tables and the FY2026 appropriations law. The series records budget authority, including the historical adjustments reported by CRS. To compare purchasing power, I converted the amounts to FY2025 dollars using the GDP price index published in the FY2027 historical tables by the #cite(<ombdeflators2026>, form: "prose"). This follows CRS's use of GDP deflation, with an updated index @harris2026[table A-1]. The GDP index measures general price changes; the salaries, equipment, and facilities in NSF-funded research may have a different cost trajectory. I use it to compare broad budget purchasing power, while an estimate of research-specific capacity would require costs matched to NSF's portfolio.

NSF's nominal appropriation rose from \$7.472 billion in FY2017 to \$8.750 billion in FY2026, an increase of 17.1%. After adjustment, the same amounts are approximately \$9.606 billion and \$8.501 billion in FY2025 dollars, a decline of 11.5%. All percentage changes use unrounded values. OMB marks the FY2026 price index as an estimate. The result through FY2025 already shows a 5.7% real decline from FY2017, so the finding of erosion predates that estimated endpoint. For FY2025 the main series retains the enacted \$9.060 billion, while the open square excludes the unavailable emergency funding. Retaining the larger amount makes the 5.7% decline conservative: on the \$8.826 billion available baseline used above, the decline is 8.1%. Congress's recent defense preserved a budget whose purchasing power had already weakened.

