Human exploration has purposes that elected officials may reasonably value. The different trajectories show why preserving an agency's total is too imprecise a claim: the activities funded determine which public purposes survive. The same question applies to NSF.

= Following the appropriation into research
NSF's 2025 terminations expose a second problem: the executive branch can change the research program after Congress funds it. Following a preliminary injunction, NSF reinstated 114 awards to 45 institutions in June @nsfpriorities2025. That was a limited set of restorations, far smaller than the more than 1,600 terminations reported earlier; it does not establish the eventual status of the remaining awards. A larger appropriation alone would not have prevented those executive decisions. The problem concerns control over implementation as well as the amount appropriated.

This is the practical significance of the course's distinction between budgeting and oversight @coursecongress2026[secs.~6.5.2–6.5.3]. Congress delegates funding decisions to an agency with scientific expertise, but agency officials possess information about individual awards that legislators cannot obtain from the topline budget. Executive priorities may also diverge from the program Congress intended to fund. This is a principal-agent problem: legislators delegate decisions but cannot readily observe whether the agency is carrying out their purposes. Oversight addresses that gap by tracing what the agency actually does with its authority. Researchers' reports of interrupted work can therefore matter even when the agency's nominal budget appears protected.

The Impoundment Control Act (ICA) provides an existing legal check on withholding budget authority. In August 2025, GAO concluded that NIH had violated it by withholding appropriated funds from obligation and expenditure without following the act's procedures @gao2025. That decision concerned NIH, not a ruling on every NSF termination. GAO also distinguishes unlawful withholding from permissible delays in carrying out a program. An individual award's cancellation and an agency's refusal to use appropriated resources raise different questions. Congress needs records of both award decisions and unspent balances to determine whether its funded program is being carried out.

The studies of funding interruptions explain why that inquiry needs to reach beyond annual totals. #cite(<tham2023>, form: "prose") finds that interrupted NIH renewals disrupt laboratory spending and that additional grants help laboratories absorb the interruption. The estimated effect on the number of papers produced was not statistically distinguishable from zero. Spending and staffing continuity can therefore reveal disruption that a publication count misses. Alongside the career effects reported by #cite(<tham2026>, form: "prose"), this supports monitoring how long research teams wait for support, especially when they have little alternative funding.

= Public reporting and advance notice
Congress already requires more than an agency total. Section 528 of the FY2026 act requires NSF to submit a spending plan within 45 days. The explanatory statement limits reductions to individual research directorates and specifies support for facilities, including astronomy priorities @congress2026[sec.~528] @appropriators2026[pp.~61–62]. My proposal would extend these requirements into a recurring public comparison of planned support with implementation. Before approving an appropriation, legislators should publish its relationship to the presidential request, historical purchasing power, and authorized objectives, identifying which commitments they are sustaining or reducing. NSF's 45-day plan should use those same program categories, then quarterly reports should reconcile planned funding with obligations, unobligated balances, and award terminations or reinstatements, giving the dates, reasons, and amounts involved.

Congress should put those reporting duties and deadlines in the enacted appropriations text. I would also require 30 days' notice to Congress and the public before NSF implements category-wide grant cancellations based on changed policy priorities. By category-wide, I mean cancellations under a common policy directive that targets awards by program, research subject, or recipient class, even if NSF issues the termination notices separately. The rule would exclude decisions based on an individual award's scientific performance and allow exceptions for fraud, immediate safety risks, or a controlling legal requirement. A statutory bar on implementing covered cancellations during the notice period would give the requirement force. Advance notice would give affected teams time to seek bridge funding before support stops, creating an opportunity to avoid the interruptions linked to job losses and laboratory disruption in the NIH studies @tham2023 @tham2026. Those findings support time to prepare; choosing 30 days as the minimum notice period is a policy judgment. Committees could question NSF officials and seek GAO review of possible withholding before teams lose support; changing the underlying law would still require legislative action.

An administration could object that the waiting period intrudes on executive management and delays a lawful change in priorities. That friction is real. I would accept a short delay for a policy that withdraws support across a class of existing awards, while leaving individual performance decisions and urgent exceptions outside the rule. The bar would expire automatically after 30 days, without requiring a committee's permission: NSF could then proceed if the cancellations were otherwise lawful. This design gives Congress time to scrutinize implementation without giving its committees a continuing veto over agency decisions.

Reporting alone cannot make legislators use their oversight powers. Scientific organizations can help supply the pressure. The American Astronomical Society encouraged members to thank Congress for the FY2026 settlement @aas2026. The course describes interest groups supplying research and mobilizing constituents @coursecongress2026[sec.~6.6.4]; public records would let those groups connect a local laboratory's interruption to an agency decision and a funded congressional commitment. The resulting question to a representative would be concrete: is this the program Congress intended to preserve?

= Conclusion
Congress protected most of NSF's previous nominal funding in FY2026. It did not restore earlier purchasing power or finance expansion at the authorized scale, and the appropriation alone could not secure continuity for individual awards. Rejecting the president's deepest cuts was a substantial exercise of the power of the purse. Calling it sufficient protection, however, lets a movable presidential request stand in for an account of what public research can do. The case for stronger reporting and notice requirements follows from the distance between the budget Congress approves and the decisions that reach laboratories. Congress can choose a smaller scientific program. It should make that choice in public and remain accountable for whether the executive carries it out.

